LINQ is lazy: Where and Select don't do anything until you loop over the result. Most people learn that as a rule. But it isn't LINQ magic. It comes straight from yield return, and once you see how yield works, LINQ's behavior stops being surprising.

Four small experiments, run for real on .NET 10 (Release build).








  1. Nothing runs until someone asks





static IEnumerable&lt;int&gt; Numbers()
{
    for (var i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"  producing {i}");
        yield return i;
    }
}

var numbers = Numbers();
Console.WriteLine("Numbers() called"

